window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-832899e58b009be2"]={"key":"ny_b1-832899e58b009be2","group":"ny_b1","uid":"832899e58b009be2","rank":65,"title":"slateford cobblers rest night uti action sheet","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"Priya, you’re the NP covering Slateford Alliance’s Cobbler’s Rest unit, and I need the new antibiotic-stewardship practice usable on nights before the survey window opens next month. The surveyor update and the Millrace Holdings pharmacy memos changed the practice, but the staff cannot work from 130 pages at 2 a.m. Our aides and LPNs still use the old “cloudy or strong-smelling urine means send a culture” shortcut, even though it does not hold across this resident population. Give the night LPN a one-page action sheet that works from the moment an aide reports a possible UTI and makes clear what has changed. The new process starts 1 October; through 15 October, qualifying calls must be recorded in both the paper overnight infection log and the new eMAR stewardship note, then from 16 October the eMAR note is the only stewardship record. Keep it clinical and operational, not a literature review or policy history, and make it readable in the medication room. I need it by 20 September. Put the finished file in the top level of your working directory using exactly the filename `night_shift_suspected_uti_action_sheet.docx`.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `night_shift_suspected_uti_action_sheet.docx` — One-page clinical action sheet for night-shift LPN response to a report of suspected urinary tract infection in a nursing-center resident. (Must be one page.; Must be readable and operational for night-shift LPNs.; Must cover both the stated transition period and the end-state documentation process.; Must identify what changed from the prior urine-testing practice.; Must be placed at the top level of the working directory with the exact required filename.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/night_shift_suspected_uti_action_sheet.docx","bytes":39695,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/44352b6dff2cce8412e817c9a5cbbed9f8b156878c5113c6bdfd7afa5f121681.docx","sha256":"44352b6dff2cce8412e817c9a5cbbed9f8b156878c5113c6bdfd7afa5f121681","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"NIGHT SHIFT  |  SUSPECTED UTI ACTION SHEET\nFor LPN response when an aide reports urine change or possible UTI • Cobbler’s Rest unit • starts 1 October\nWHAT CHANGED  |  Cloudy, dark, sediment-filled, strong/foul-smelling urine alone is nonspecific. It does NOT trigger reflex dipstick, culture, or antibiotics. Assess the resident first; testing and treatment require an authorized prescriber.\n\n1  FIRST 90 SECONDS\n• Go to resident; verify identity and what aide saw. Do not collect urine yet.\n• Check acute instability: markedly abnormal vital signs, hypotension, respiratory distress, new severe hypoxia, rigors with rapid decline, or rapidly worsening condition.\n• If severely unstable: activate facility emergency response/EMS, stabilize and transfer per policy. Emergency care takes priority—do not delay for a specimen.\n• If stable, obtain T, BP, pulse, RR, and O₂ saturation; compare cognition, behavior, function, and continence with usual baseline.\n• Ask onset, timing, and progression (new, worsening, improving, unchanged).\n2  FOCUSED ASSESSMENT\n• Localizing urinary symptoms: acute dysuria (new painful urination), new/worsening urgency or frequency, suprapubic pain, gross hematuria, CVA/flank tenderness, urinary incontinence.\n• Fever = >100°F (37.9°C) OR ≥2.4°F (1.5°C) above baseline.\n• New mental-status or functional change? Assess other causes; it is not proof of UTI by itself.\n• Check dehydration, constipation/retention, medication effects, hypoxia, pain, and abnormal blood glucose.\n3  CHOOSE THE BRANCH\n• Is an indwelling urinary catheter present? Route below; do not use the non-catheter rule for catheterized residents.\n• NO CATHETER: notify on-call prescriber for acute dysuria alone OR fever threshold PLUS ≥1 new localizing urinary symptom. Fever alone, odor/cloudiness, or chronic symptoms do not meet this pathway.\n• CATHETER: fever, rigors, new delirium, or new CVA tenderness → prompt clinical escalation. Appearance/odor alone does not.\n• Bacteriuria/pyuria or a positive urine result alone does not establish symptomatic UTI. Without attributable urinary/systemic findings this is asymptomatic bacteriuria: default no urine testing and no antibiotics. Pregnancy or upcoming invasive urologic procedure → provider review.\n• Stable and no escalation criteria: offer fluids if not restricted/aspiration risk, comfort care and toileting; observe.\n4  CALL THE PRESCRIBER (SBAR)\n• Situation: concern, onset/progression, current vitals and symptoms.\n• Background: catheter status; allergies + reaction; current medications; renal-function information; recent antibiotics; prior cultures; resistant-organism history.\n• Assessment: baseline comparison, localizing findings, alternatives considered, and stability.\n• Request: ask whether to examine, order UA/culture, treat, or continue monitoring; document response and orders. LPN does not diagnose, order tests, or start antibiotics independently.\n5  SPECIMEN & MEDICATION SAFETY\n• Collect urine only when clinically indicated AND ordered under facility process—not automatically after an aide report.\n• If ordered, collect before first antibiotic dose when feasible; never delay emergency stabilization or urgent treatment.\n• No catheter: labeled clean-catch using clean technique; prompt transport or approved storage.\n• Catheter: disinfect sampling port and collect from port—NEVER drainage bag. If long-standing catheter remains necessary, coordinate with provider about replacement before culture when clinically appropriate; do not replace automatically.\n• Never start leftover, borrowed, or unconfirmed standing antibiotics. Use a current resident-specific order.\n• After administration, monitor allergy/adverse reaction and clinical improvement or deterioration. Persistent/worsening symptoms, new instability, inability to take treatment, or adverse reaction → re-contact provider; emergency response for severe instability.\n• No routine post-treatment UA/culture/test-of-cure when clinically improved and asymptomatic unless specifically indicated and ordered.\n6  RECORD • REASSESS • HAND OFF\n• Document report received; assessment/vitals; baseline comparison; onset/progression; provider contact + response; orders; specimen source/time/handling; actions; resident response.\n• Set a time-bound reassessment (e.g., 2–4 h or provider-specified). Contact sooner for worsening symptoms, new instability, inability to take treatment, or reaction.\n• Handoff unresolved concerns, pending results, and next reassessment time.\nDOCUMENTATION TRANSITION  |  Through 15 October: qualifying calls go in BOTH the paper overnight infection log AND the eMAR stewardship note. From 16 October: the eMAR stewardship note is the SOLE stewardship record (no duplicate paper log).\nUse resident-specific orders and facility emergency policy. When uncertain, escalate.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required DOCX exists under the exact filename and opens as a valid, editable Word document.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"The submitted file and its internal DOCX structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename, successful opening, and that the file is a genuine DOCX rather than a renamed or corrupt file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete action sheet renders on exactly one page without clipped or overflowing content.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX page count and page boundaries.","grader_hint":"Render using a standard office suite and verify that all intended content remains on one page."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The rendered sheet is comfortably legible in medication-room use, with readable type, adequate contrast, and no reliance on cramped continuous prose.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Visual inspection of the rendered page.","grader_hint":"Judge whether an LPN could scan the sheet under routine workplace conditions without zooming or deciphering tiny text."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sheet clearly identifies its audience as night-shift nursing staff and its purpose as action on a suspected UTI report.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Title, subtitle, or opening instruction in the document.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit audience and suspected-UTI purpose language rather than a generic infection-control title."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sheet presents an unmistakable starting point for the moment an aide reports a urine change or possible UTI.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"The first operational instruction and the document's visual flow.","grader_hint":"Confirm that a night LPN can immediately identify the first action without reading background material."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition instruction requires qualifying calls to be recorded in both the paper overnight infection log and the eMAR stewardship note through 15 October.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Transition-period documentation instruction.","grader_hint":"Both destinations and the through-15-October duration must be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The end-state instruction states that from 16 October the eMAR stewardship note is the sole stewardship record.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"End-state documentation instruction.","grader_hint":"Look for the date, the eMAR destination, and a clear discontinuation of duplicate paper stewardship logging."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sheet visibly communicates the practice change: urine appearance or odor alone no longer triggers reflex dipstick testing, culture collection, or antibiotic treatment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Prominent change notice or instruction in the action flow.","grader_hint":"Award only if the old shortcut and the operational consequence of the change are both clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sheet names acute instability findings relevant to suspected infection, including markedly abnormal vital signs or rapidly worsening clinical condition.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Urgent or emergency escalation content.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent clinically meaningful wording; a bare instruction to check whether the resident is unstable is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sheet directs immediate use of the facility emergency response or emergency medical services for a severely unstable resident rather than routine UTI workflow.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Emergency branch of the action sheet.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the emergency action is direct and does not merely say to leave a message for the next shift."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The emergency branch makes urgent stabilization or transfer take precedence over obtaining a urine specimen.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Relationship between emergency action and specimen instructions.","grader_hint":"The document must not require completion of urine collection before emergency escalation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The initial assessment calls for temperature, blood pressure, pulse, respiratory rate, and oxygen saturation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Initial resident assessment content.","grader_hint":"All five measurements must be present, though common abbreviations are acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The assessment directs the LPN to compare cognition, behavior, function, and continence with the resident's usual baseline.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Baseline-comparison instruction.","grader_hint":"Award when the document distinguishes an acute change from a resident's chronic or usual status."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The LPN is instructed to establish and document symptom onset, timing, and progression.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Assessment or documentation prompt.","grader_hint":"Look for when the change began and whether it is worsening, improving, or unchanged."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Acute dysuria is identified as a high-value localizing urinary symptom rather than being grouped with urine appearance alone.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Symptom assessment or decision criteria.","grader_hint":"The document should specifically distinguish new painful urination from nonspecific urine changes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The local urinary assessment includes new or worsening urgency, frequency, suprapubic pain, gross hematuria, costovertebral-angle or flank tenderness, and urinary incontinence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"List of localizing urinary symptoms.","grader_hint":"Accept clinically equivalent terms, but all six symptom categories must be represented."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Fever is defined as greater than 100°F/37.9°C or at least 2.4°F/1.5°C above the resident's baseline temperature.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Temperature threshold stated in the action sheet.","grader_hint":"Accept rounding within ±0.1°F or ±0.1°C. Both the absolute threshold and rise-above-baseline alternative must appear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For a resident without an indwelling catheter, the sheet links provider notification or antibiotic consideration to acute dysuria alone, or to fever plus at least one new localizing urinary symptom.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Non-catheter decision pathway.","grader_hint":"Check the AND/OR relationship carefully. The sheet must not imply that fever alone or a nonspecific urine change satisfies the pathway."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sheet explicitly determines whether an indwelling urinary catheter is present and routes catheterized residents through a distinct assessment branch.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Decision flow or catheter-status prompt.","grader_hint":"A catheter mention buried only in specimen technique does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The catheterized-resident branch recognizes fever, rigors, new delirium, or new costovertebral-angle tenderness as findings that warrant clinical escalation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Catheter-associated symptom criteria.","grader_hint":"All four finding categories must be represented; equivalent clinical terminology is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Cloudy urine, dark urine, sediment, and strong or foul odor are identified as nonspecific findings when they occur without compatible symptoms or systemic signs.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Nonspecific-findings or do-not-reflex-test content.","grader_hint":"All four common appearance or odor observations should be addressed, and their insufficiency when isolated must be clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A new mental-status or functional change prompts assessment for other causes and is not presented as proof of UTI by itself.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Delirium or functional-change pathway.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the document neither ignores acute delirium nor treats it as automatically urinary in origin."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sheet explains in plain operational language that bacteriuria without attributable urinary symptoms or systemic infection findings is asymptomatic bacteriuria.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Asymptomatic-bacteriuria instruction.","grader_hint":"The formal term or an unmistakably equivalent definition must be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sheet states that bacteriuria, pyuria, or a positive urine result does not by itself establish symptomatic UTI.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Interpretation guidance for urine testing.","grader_hint":"Award only if both positive microbiology and pyuria are prevented from being treated as stand-alone diagnoses."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Absent compatible symptoms or systemic findings, the default pathway avoids urine testing and antibiotic treatment for asymptomatic bacteriuria.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Relationship among symptom assessment, testing, and treatment instructions.","grader_hint":"The no-test/no-treat result must follow from the absence of compatible findings, not from a blanket ban on clinical evaluation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sheet preserves provider review for recognized exceptions to the usual asymptomatic-bacteriuria rule, such as pregnancy or an upcoming invasive urologic procedure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Exception or special-circumstance instruction.","grader_hint":"Accept concise wording that tells the LPN to notify the provider rather than independently applying the usual no-treatment rule."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sheet keeps diagnosis, urine-test ordering, and antibiotic initiation within authorized prescriber decisions rather than assigning them independently to the LPN.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Role and authority language throughout the action flow.","grader_hint":"Check that the LPN assesses, escalates, collects ordered specimens, administers orders, and monitors without being told to diagnose or prescribe."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Provider-contact thresholds are concrete enough to distinguish immediate emergency escalation, prompt on-call notification, and monitoring with handoff.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Escalation levels and decision flow.","grader_hint":"The three dispositions must be operationally distinguishable; vague language such as 'notify as needed' is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The provider call prompt supplies a concise SBAR-style report containing the current concern, relevant background, assessment findings, and requested decision or recommendation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Call script, communication checklist, or equivalent prompts.","grader_hint":"The letters SBAR need not appear if all four communication functions are clearly supported."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The provider-call preparation includes medication allergies and the nature of any prior reaction.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Pre-call or antibiotic-safety checklist.","grader_hint":"A generic instruction to check the chart is insufficient unless allergies and reactions are named."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The call preparation includes current medications and available renal-function information relevant to safe antibiotic selection and dosing.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Provider communication or medication-safety content.","grader_hint":"Look for both medication review and kidney or renal status; exact laboratory values need not be mandated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The LPN is prompted to report recent antibiotic exposure, prior urine-culture results, and any known resistant-organism history.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Background information for provider communication.","grader_hint":"All three stewardship-relevant history elements should be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment identifies plausible alternative causes of nonspecific change, including dehydration, constipation or retention, medication effects, hypoxia, pain, and abnormal blood glucose.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Alternate-cause assessment prompts.","grader_hint":"Accept clinically equivalent wording, but all six categories must be represented."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For a stable resident who does not meet escalation criteria, the sheet gives safe supportive actions such as offering fluids when not restricted, comfort care, toileting, and observation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Stable-resident monitoring branch.","grader_hint":"Supportive care must include a safety qualifier for fluid restrictions or aspiration risk and must not substitute for escalation when the resident is unstable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The monitoring branch requires a time-bound reassessment and identifies findings that trigger earlier provider contact.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Reassessment instruction and escalation triggers.","grader_hint":"A specific interval or resident-specific scheduled time is acceptable; 'monitor' without timing or triggers is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Unresolved concerns, pending results, and the next reassessment time are included in shift handoff.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Handoff instruction.","grader_hint":"All three continuity elements must be passed forward rather than only noting that a call occurred."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Urine collection occurs only when clinically indicated and ordered under the applicable facility process, not automatically after an aide report.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Relationship between assessment, provider decision, and specimen collection.","grader_hint":"Confirm that assessment precedes collection and that the LPN is not instructed to reflexively send urine."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"When a specimen is ordered, the sheet calls for collection before the first antibiotic dose when feasible, while preserving the emergency exception.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Specimen timing instruction.","grader_hint":"Both the preferred sequence and the instruction not to delay urgent treatment must be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For a non-catheterized resident, the sheet calls for a properly labeled clean-catch specimen using clean technique and prompt transport or approved storage.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Non-catheter specimen instruction.","grader_hint":"Check for specimen integrity, resident identification, and timely handling without expecting system-specific click steps."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For an indwelling catheter, the sheet requires sampling from the disinfected sampling port and explicitly excludes collection from the drainage bag.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Catheter specimen instruction.","grader_hint":"Both the approved source and the prohibited drainage-bag source must be stated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For a long-standing catheter that remains necessary, the sheet prompts coordination with the provider for replacement before culture collection when clinically appropriate.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Catheter-management instruction.","grader_hint":"The LPN should not be told to perform unauthorized replacement automatically; the instruction should preserve an ordered, clinically appropriate decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sheet forbids starting leftover, borrowed, or unconfirmed standing antibiotics and requires a current resident-specific order.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Antibiotic initiation instruction.","grader_hint":"Equivalent wording is acceptable if it clearly prevents unsanctioned antibiotic administration."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"After an antibiotic is administered, the sheet directs monitoring for allergy or adverse reaction and for clinical improvement or deterioration.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Post-administration monitoring content.","grader_hint":"Both medication safety and clinical response should be addressed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required clinical record captures the report received, assessment findings, baseline comparison, provider contact and response, orders, specimen details, actions taken, and resident response.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Documentation checklist or prompts.","grader_hint":"All eight documentation categories must be represented, though they may be grouped compactly."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sheet states that routine post-treatment urinalysis, culture, or test-of-cure is not obtained for a clinically improved asymptomatic resident unless specifically indicated and ordered.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Follow-up testing instruction.","grader_hint":"The instruction should prevent automatic retesting while preserving individualized provider decisions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Persistent or worsening symptoms, new instability, inability to take ordered treatment, or an adverse reaction produce a clear re-escalation path.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Follow-up and re-escalation content.","grader_hint":"All four triggers should result in prompt clinical contact or emergency action appropriate to severity."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Clinical wording avoids false certainty, including claims that absence of fever rules out UTI or that a positive culture proves symptomatic infection.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"All diagnostic and decision language in the sheet.","grader_hint":"Review the entire document for categorical statements that conflict with the qualified assessment pathways."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Information is prioritized around what the LPN must do during the first roughly ninety seconds, with detail deferred until the relevant decision branch.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Information hierarchy and action flow.","grader_hint":"Judge whether emergency screening, focused assessment, and the no-reflex-culture message are immediately visible rather than buried."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A night-shift LPN can independently execute the sheet without asking its author to define a threshold, choose the next branch, or determine what to communicate.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"The complete rendered action sheet.","grader_hint":"Walk through at least three cases: isolated cloudy urine, acute dysuria without a catheter, and an unstable catheterized resident."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The action sheet remains practically editable and reusable, with substantive text stored as document content rather than a single flattened image.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"DOCX object and text structure.","grader_hint":"Inspect whether text can be selected and edited and whether routine future date or wording updates are feasible."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A nurse practitioner is implementing an authorized antibiotic-stewardship practice change at a single nursing-center unit. The artifact will be used by night-shift LPNs responding to possible UTI reports when on-site clinical support is limited.","difficulty_sources":["Compressing clinically material stewardship guidance into a genuinely usable one-page night-shift artifact.","Distinguishing symptomatic UTI from asymptomatic bacteriuria without creating unsafe categorical rules.","Designing separate catheter and non-catheter pathways while preserving scope-of-practice boundaries.","Supporting both a temporary dual-documentation period and the final eMAR-only process.","Replacing an entrenched urine-appearance shortcut with a rapid assessment workflow suitable for a 2 a.m. call."],"authoring_plan":{"solver_instance":"An experienced nurse practitioner must convert accepted long-term-care UTI and asymptomatic-bacteriuria stewardship practice into a one-page action sheet that a night LPN can execute immediately.","behavior_specification":["Begin from an aide report and rapidly separate instability from a stable nonspecific urine observation.","Make the prior appearance-and-odor shortcut visibly obsolete without dismissing a resident's acute clinical change.","Use distinct non-catheter and indwelling-catheter pathways.","Keep diagnosis, test ordering, and prescribing with an authorized prescriber while giving the LPN concrete assessment, escalation, specimen, administration, monitoring, handoff, and documentation actions.","Represent both the temporary dual-documentation period and the later single-record end state exactly as specified in the request.","Fit all material content on one readable page and keep the source editable."],"clinical_relationships_for_gold":["Emergency instability overrides routine suspected-UTI assessment and specimen collection.","For a resident without an indwelling catheter, accepted minimum antibiotic-consideration criteria are acute dysuria alone, or fever greater than 100°F/37.9°C or at least 2.4°F/1.5°C above baseline plus at least one new localizing urinary symptom.","Localizing symptoms include new or worsening urgency, frequency, suprapubic pain, gross hematuria, costovertebral-angle tenderness, and urinary incontinence.","For a resident with an indwelling catheter, fever, rigors, new delirium, or new costovertebral-angle tenderness warrants clinical escalation; urine appearance alone does not.","Cloudiness, darkness, sediment, and odor are nonspecific and do not independently justify dipstick testing, culture, or antibiotics.","Bacteriuria or pyuria without attributable symptoms is not symptomatic UTI; a positive urine result alone does not establish infection.","The default for asymptomatic bacteriuria is no screening culture and no antibiotic treatment, while recognized exceptions such as pregnancy or an invasive urologic procedure are referred to the prescriber.","A mental-status or functional change requires evaluation for alternate causes and does not become proof of UTI merely because bacteriuria is common in this population.","If urine is ordered, collect it before antibiotics when feasible, but never delay stabilization or urgent treatment.","A catheter specimen comes from a disinfected sampling port, never the drainage bag; replacement of a long-standing necessary catheter is coordinated rather than performed automatically without authority.","Stable residents who do not meet escalation criteria receive safe supportive care, a time-bound reassessment, documentation, and handoff.","Routine test-of-cure is avoided after clinical improvement unless individualized circumstances and an authorized order indicate otherwise."],"material_cases_to_prove":[{"case":"An aide reports cloudy, strong-smelling urine in a stable resident with no new symptoms.","intended_verdict":"Assess the resident and alternatives, provide safe supportive care as appropriate, do not reflexively dip, culture, or seek antibiotics, set reassessment, document, and hand off."},{"case":"A non-catheterized resident has new painful urination but no fever.","intended_verdict":"Perform the focused assessment and contact the on-call prescriber because acute dysuria alone satisfies the non-catheter minimum pathway."},{"case":"A non-catheterized resident has fever and new urgency.","intended_verdict":"Contact the prescriber with a structured report; collect urine only if indicated and ordered, preferably before antibiotics."},{"case":"A catheterized resident develops rigors and new delirium.","intended_verdict":"Escalate promptly, screen for instability, and use the catheter-specific pathway rather than the non-catheter symptom rule."},{"case":"A resident is hypotensive and rapidly deteriorating while urine collection is pending.","intended_verdict":"Activate emergency response and do not delay stabilization or transfer to obtain urine."},{"case":"A stable resident has pyuria and a positive culture but no attributable symptoms.","intended_verdict":"Do not label the result as symptomatic UTI or automatically treat; refer recognized exceptions to the prescriber."}],"required_local_vocabulary":["paper overnight infection log","eMAR stewardship note","15 October","16 October"],"gold_validation":["Render the DOCX offline with a standard office suite and confirm exactly one page with no clipping.","Walk every rubric item against the rendered document and editable source.","Run the material cases through the action flow and confirm each reaches the intended verdict without relying on information outside the sheet.","Confirm that the transition instructions do not accidentally continue duplicate paper stewardship logging after the end-state date.","Confirm that no wording equates cloudy urine, pyuria, bacteriuria, or a positive culture with symptomatic infection by itself."]},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"night_uti_action_sheet","filename":"night_shift_suspected_uti_action_sheet.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"One-page clinical action sheet for night-shift LPN response to a report of suspected urinary tract infection in a nursing-center resident.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be one page.","Must be readable and operational for night-shift LPNs.","Must cover both the stated transition period and the end-state documentation process.","Must identify what changed from the prior urine-testing practice.","Must be placed at the top level of the working directory with the exact required filename."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"document_information","work_product":"professional_report","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","sector":"healthcare_social_assistance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution should be authored by an experienced long-term-care nurse practitioner and independently reviewed by a long-term-care infection-prevention or antibiotic-stewardship clinician. Reviewers must verify clinical safety, LPN scope clarity, transition-date accuracy, one-page usability, and consistency of every decision branch.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The action sheet supports frontline recognition, escalation, specimen stewardship, and documentation; it does not replace resident-specific orders or the facility emergency-response policy.","The local transition dates and documentation destinations are scenario-specific.","The artifact cannot encode every atypical presentation or special population and therefore preserves provider review and emergency escalation."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:32:17+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:32:17+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:32:17+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:32:17+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['R06']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:32:17+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/832899e58b009be2","declared_deliverables":["night_shift_suspected_uti_action_sheet.docx"]};
